From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:55:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] phy: qcom: qmp-combo: Add the Glymur USB4 and TBT3 PHY
 sequences

The preliminary USB4 support programs a USB4 PHY from per-block serdes,
lane and PCS tables. Glymur has no such tables, so its combo PHY
registers no USB4 PHY and a USB4 router cannot use the port.

The Glymur USB4 and TBT3 start-up is one ordered sequence over the
whole combo PHY (common block, USB4 serdes and PCS, both lane blocks),
followed by an RX IVCM calibration check that resets the USB4 PCS and
retries once when the PHY asks for recalibration. Add the two sequences
as tables of offsets into the complete PHY register space and a
configure_usb4_phy() hook that runs them in place of the generic tables:

- the common block and the USB3 PCS are put into software reset before
  the sequence, and the common block and the USB4 PCS are released and
  started after it; the DP tunnelling clock bit of PHY_MODE_CTRL, which
  the first record rewrites, is preserved;
- the lane RX equalisation (RX_MODE_RATE3_B4) is board specific and
  comes from the new qcom,usb4-rx-eq property; USB4 is refused when a
  board does not provide it;
- the USB4 serdes and PCS offsets and the USB4 always-on clamp register
  are added to the Glymur layout. Without the clamp entry, USB4 mode
  entry would set CLAMP_EN at offset 0 of the always-on block, which is
  the USB3 clamp.

The USB4 PHY is now also created for configurations that provide the
hook instead of tables.

The values match what the platform's Windows driver programs on the
Surface Laptop 8.

Assisted-by: Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
